{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794350274755,"sessionID":"ses_e386cd387ffeGJ3KEnYGDpYp5r","part":{"id":"prt_1c79c58af001HdWioF0kAkrrU3","messageID":"msg_1c79c4038001g1WP4agK3zvS1T","sessionID":"ses_e386cd387ffeGJ3KEnYGDpYp5r","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794350274756,"sessionID":"ses_e386cd387ffeGJ3KEnYGDpYp5r","part":{"id":"prt_1c79c58b100158kmCyXwie2ic3","messageID":"msg_1c79c4038001g1WP4agK3zvS1T","sessionID":"ses_e386cd387ffeGJ3KEnYGDpYp5r","type":"text","text":"<think>No root, no apt. So no browser testing available. I'll rely on careful code review plus a jsdom-based smoke test maybe. Let me consider installing `jsdom` + `vitest` from npm to render the App and check basic interactions. That's feasible: npm registry works. But React 19 + testing library adds work. \n\nA lighter approach: use `jsdom` with a script that loads the built bundle and simulates DOM? Complex.\n\nAlternative: install `vitest` + `@testing-library/react` + `jsdom` to write a few smoke tests. This is valuable for verifying the frontend actually renders and the flows work (register → add item → receive stock). It also catches runtime errors. Let me do it — npm install is fast.\n\nActually, the React Testing Library requires jsdom environment. Let me install:\n- vitest\n- jsdom\n- @testing-library/react\n- @testing-library/user-event (optional)\n- @testing-library/jest-dom (optional)\n\nAnd write a test file `src/__tests__/app.test.tsx` that:\n1. Mocks fetch with an in-memory API implementation (or better: run the real Flask backend and point fetch at it? Node's fetch can hit http://127.0.0.1:8123 — but the app uses relative URLs like \"/api/items\" which need a base URL. I could set `globalThis.fetch` to prepend the base URL and forward to the real Flask server! That gives a true integration test of the frontend against the real backend.\n\nThat's a great approach: in the test setup, wrap fetch: `const original = globalThis.fetch; globalThis.fetch = (input, init) => { const url = typeof input === \"string\" ? new URL(input, BASE) : input; return original(url, init); }`.\n\nThen run vitest with jsdom, start the Flask server on a port, and test the full flow. Let me do that.\n\nTests to write:\n1. Renders auth panel when signed out; register a user → header shows \"Signed in as X\".\n2. Add item via dialog → appears in table.\n3. Low stock filter shows the item.\n4. Open item page → receive stock → quantity updates; movement history shows entry.\n5. Delete asks for confirmation.\n\nThis is a decent investment but gives confidence. Let me do it.\n\nNote: vitest + jsdom + @testing-library/react versions compatible with React 19: @testing-library/react v16 supports React 19.\n\nLet me install.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794350274737,"end":1794350274752}}}
